body:
@DeeJayLSP we already acknowledge that mimalloc is faster than our allocator. But what's even faster is not allocating memory at all. A contrived example showing that allocating memory with mimalloc is faster than before doesn't change the fact that it won't make a difference if we stop doing run time allocations. 

We need to see the final impact of mimalloc before we go ahead and merge it. It's a massive dependency that comes with a lot of its own problems (i.e. it doesn't play nice with GC languages). So it's not a good temporary workaround while we do the actual optimizations. 

Our time work be much better spent doing actual optimizations before trying to apply a band aid over the existing problems. If, after doing everything we can, mimalloc still has a good benefit then we can evaluate merging it and putting in all the work required to solve the problems that it introduces
